Tic-Tac-Toe Game — Low-Level System Design (LLD) in Java
For an LLD interview, the goal is to design a Tic-Tac-Toe game using object-oriented programming, clean class responsibilities, and extensible design.

We will design a 3×3 game for two players, where players take turns placing X and O. The first player to get three symbols in a row, column, or diagonal wins. If the board fills up without a winner, the game ends in a draw.
1. Requirements
Functional requirements
- Two players can play a game.
- Players take turns making moves.
- A player cannot make a move in an occupied cell.
- Invalid moves should be rejected.
- The game detects a win or draw.
- The game prevents further moves after it finishes.
Object-oriented design goals
- Encapsulate the board and player data.
- Separate game management from win detection.
- Make it easy to extend the game to different board sizes or winning rules.
2. Class design
TicTacToeGame

Controls turns, validates moves, and manages game status


Board

Stores cells and places symbols


Player

Stores player name and symbol


WinningStrategy

Determines whether a move wins


GameStatus

IN_PROGRESS, X_WON, O_WON, DRAW




3. Complete Java implementation
The following code is interview-friendly and uses an interface for the winning strategy, so the winning logic can be changed independently of the game.

import java.util.*;

// Represents the symbol placed on the board
enum Symbol {
    X, O, EMPTY
}

// Represents the current game status
enum GameStatus {
    IN_PROGRESS,
    X_WON,
    O_WON,
    DRAW
}

// Represents a player
class Player {
    private final String name;
    private final Symbol symbol;

    public Player(String name, Symbol symbol) {
        this.name = name;
        this.symbol = symbol;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public Symbol getSymbol() {
        return symbol;
    }
}

// Represents a move on the board
class Move {
    private final int row;
    private final int col;
    private final Player player;

    public Move(int row, int col, Player player) {
        this.row = row;
        this.col = col;
        this.player = player;
    }

    public int getRow() {
        return row;
    }

    public int getCol() {
        return col;
    }

    public Player getPlayer() {
        return player;
    }
}

// Manages the board
class Board {
    private final Symbol[][] grid;
    private final int size;
    private int movesCount;

    public Board(int size) {
        this.size = size;
        this.grid = new Symbol[size][size];
        this.movesCount = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            Arrays.fill(grid[i], Symbol.EMPTY);
        }
    }

    public boolean isValidMove(int row, int col) {
        return row >= 0 && row < size
                && col >= 0 && col < size
                && grid[row][col] == Symbol.EMPTY;
    }

    public void placeSymbol(Move move) {
        int row = move.getRow();
        int col = move.getCol();

        if (!isValidMove(row, col)) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid move!");
        }

        grid[row][col] = move.getPlayer().getSymbol();
        movesCount++;
    }

    public Symbol getCell(int row, int col) {
        return grid[row][col];
    }

    public int getSize() {
        return size;
    }

    public boolean isFull() {
        return movesCount == size * size;
    }

    public void display() {
        for (Symbol[] row : grid) {
            for (Symbol cell : row) {
                System.out.print(
                    (cell == Symbol.EMPTY ? "-" : cell) + " "
                );
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}

// Strategy interface for checking a win
interface WinningStrategy {
    boolean checkWinner(Board board, Move move);
}

// Standard Tic-Tac-Toe winning rules
class StandardWinningStrategy implements WinningStrategy {

    @Override
    public boolean checkWinner(Board board, Move move) {
        int row = move.getRow();
        int col = move.getCol();
        Symbol symbol = move.getPlayer().getSymbol();
        int n = board.getSize();

        // Check the entire row
        boolean rowWin = true;
        for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
            if (board.getCell(row, j) != symbol) {
                rowWin = false;
                break;
            }
        }

        if (rowWin) {
            return true;
        }

        // Check the entire column
        boolean colWin = true;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            if (board.getCell(i, col) != symbol) {
                colWin = false;
                break;
            }
        }

        if (colWin) {
            return true;
        }

        // Check the main diagonal, if applicable
        if (row == col) {
            boolean diagonalWin = true;

            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                if (board.getCell(i, i) != symbol) {
                    diagonalWin = false;
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (diagonalWin) {
                return true;
            }
        }

        // Check the opposite diagonal, if applicable
        if (row + col == n - 1) {
            boolean diagonalWin = true;

            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                if (board.getCell(i, n - 1 - i) != symbol) {
                    diagonalWin = false;
                    break;
                }
            }

            if (diagonalWin) {
                return true;
            }
        }

        return false;
    }
}

// Controls the entire game
class TicTacToeGame {
    private final Board board;
    private final Player[] players;
    private final WinningStrategy winningStrategy;

    private int currentPlayerIndex;
    private GameStatus status;

    public TicTacToeGame(
            Player player1,
            Player player2,
            WinningStrategy winningStrategy) {

        if (player1.getSymbol() != Symbol.X
                || player2.getSymbol() != Symbol.O) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "Player 1 must be X and Player 2 must be O"
            );
        }

        this.board = new Board(3);
        this.players = new Player[]{player1, player2};
        this.winningStrategy = winningStrategy;
        this.currentPlayerIndex = 0;
        this.status = GameStatus.IN_PROGRESS;
    }

    public void makeMove(int row, int col) {
        if (status != GameStatus.IN_PROGRESS) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Game has finished!");
        }

        Player currentPlayer = players[currentPlayerIndex];
        Move move = new Move(row, col, currentPlayer);

        // Validate and place the symbol
        board.placeSymbol(move);

        // Check whether this move wins
        if (winningStrategy.checkWinner(board, move)) {
            status = currentPlayer.getSymbol() == Symbol.X
                    ? GameStatus.X_WON
                    : GameStatus.O_WON;

            System.out.println(currentPlayer.getName() + " wins!");
        } else if (board.isFull()) {
            status = GameStatus.DRAW;
            System.out.println("Game is a draw!");
        } else {
            // Switch to the other player
            currentPlayerIndex = 1 - currentPlayerIndex;
        }

        board.display();
    }

    public GameStatus getStatus() {
        return status;
    }

    public void displayBoard() {
        board.display();
    }
}

// Entry point
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Player player1 = new Player("Alice", Symbol.X);
        Player player2 = new Player("Bob", Symbol.O);

        WinningStrategy strategy =
                new StandardWinningStrategy();

        TicTacToeGame game =
                new TicTacToeGame(player1, player2, strategy);

        game.makeMove(0, 0); // Alice: X
        game.makeMove(1, 0); // Bob: O
        game.makeMove(0, 1); // Alice: X
        game.makeMove(1, 1); // Bob: O
        game.makeMove(0, 2); // Alice wins
    }
}



Save the code in Main.java and run it. The sample game ends when Alice completes the first row.
4. Explanation of every class
1. Symbol and GameStatus
enum Symbol {
    X, O, EMPTY
}

enum GameStatus {
    IN_PROGRESS, X_WON, O_WON, DRAW
}


- Symbol represents what a cell contains.
- GameStatus tells us whether the game is running, won by X, won by O, or drawn.
Using enums avoids error-prone string comparisons such as "x" versus "X".
2. Player
class Player {
    private final String name;
    private final Symbol symbol;
}


Each player has a name and a symbol.

For example:
- Alice → X
- Bob → O
The fields are private to enforce encapsulation, and final because a player's identity and symbol do not change during the game.
3. Move
A Move represents one player's attempt to occupy one cell.

For example:
new Move(0, 2, player1);


This means player 1 wants to place X at row 0, column 2. Java array indexes start at zero.
