6. Which design patterns are used?
Pattern / principle	Where it is used	Why
Strategy pattern	WinningStrategy	Allows alternative winning rules
Encapsulation	Board, Player	Protects internal data
Single Responsibility Principle	Separate Board, Player, Move, and game classes	Each class has a focused responsibility
Dependency injection	TicTacToeGame constructor	The winning strategy is provided from outside

Why use the Strategy pattern?

Suppose you later want a different winning rule. You can implement another WinningStrategy and pass it to the game without rewriting TicTacToeGame.

The current implementation uses only StandardWinningStrategy; it does not need a Factory pattern or Singleton pattern.
7. Time and space complexity
Let \(N\) be the board dimension.
- Move validation: \(O(1)\)
- Place a symbol: \(O(1)\)
- Winner detection: \(O(N)\), because at most one row, one column, and two diagonals are checked.
- Board space: \(O(N^2)\)
For the standard 3×3 game, winner detection takes constant time because the board size is fixed.
